Title: The Call of the Wild
Credit: Written by
Author: Jack London
Source: Adapted from the novel by Jack London
Draft date: 7/31/2026

FADE IN:

INT. CABIN - NIGHT

A grizzled old prospector, the NARRATOR, sits by a roaring fire. He holds an open book but speaks from memory, his eyes on the flames.

NARRATOR
Old longings nomadic leap,
Chafing at custom's chain;
Again from its brumal sleep
Wakens the ferine strain.

In the fall of 1897, thousands of men rushed north for gold. They wanted heavy dogs with strong muscles and warm coats.

EXT. JUDGE MILLER'S PLACE - DAY

A magnificent estate basks in California sun. BUCK, a 140-pound St. Bernard-Scotch shepherd mix with glossy coat and royal bearing, rules the wide lawns, stables, and orchards.

Buck plunges into the swimming tank with the Judge's sons, escorts the daughters on rambles, lies at the Judge's feet by the library fire at night.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Buck had lived the life of a sated aristocrat. He was king over all things on Judge Miller's place.

MANUEL, a gardener's helper, approaches with a rope. Buck trusts him and follows into the orchard.

EXT. COLLEGE PARK FLAG STATION - NIGHT

A solitary lantern swings. The STRANGER waits. Money chinks between him and Manuel.

STRANGER
You might wrap up the goods before you deliver 'm.

Manuel slips the rope around Buck's neck under the collar.

MANUEL
Twist it, an' you'll choke 'm plentee.

Buck growls as the rope tightens. He springs. The stranger grapples him, twists, throws him down. The rope chokes off his breath. Buck's eyes glaze. They fling him into the baggage car.

INT. BAGGAGE CAR - NIGHT

The train shrieks. Buck wakes in rage, attacks the stranger. Jaws close on the hand. The man chokes him unconscious again.

STRANGER
Yep, has fits. I'm takin' 'm up for the boss to 'Frisco.

INT. SALOON SHED - NIGHT

The stranger nurses a mangled hand wrapped in bloody cloth. The SALOON-KEEPER counts money.

SALOON-KEEPER
That makes a hundred and fifty. He's worth it.

They file off Buck's brass collar, rope him, and fling him into a crate. Buck lies nursing wrath and wounded pride through the long night. The saloon-keeper's face peers in by candlelight each time the door rattles. Buck's joyful bark twists into a savage growl.

EXT. SEATTLE BACK YARD - DAY

Four men carry the crate into a high-walled yard. A stout MAN IN THE RED SWEATER signs for it. Buck hurls himself against the bars.

The man smiles grimly, fetches a hatchet and club. He pries open the crate.

MAN IN THE RED SWEATER
Now, you red-eyed devil.

Buck launches 140 pounds of fury. The club strikes mid-air. Buck crashes down. He charges again and again. Each time the club smashes him. Blood flows from nose, mouth, ears. A final shrewd blow on the nose drops him senseless.

MAN IN THE RED SWEATER
He's no slouch at dog-breakin'.

Later, Buck's senses return. The man pats the head he pounded.

MAN IN THE RED SWEATER
'Answers to the name of Buck.' Well, Buck, my boy, we've had our little ruction. You've learned your place, and I know mine. Be a good dog and all'll go well. Be a bad dog, and I'll whale the stuffin' outa you. Understand?

Buck drinks eagerly, eats raw meat from the man's hand. He is beaten but not broken.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
That club was a revelation. It was his introduction to the reign of primitive law. A man with a club was a lawgiver, a master to be obeyed.

INT. CABIN - NIGHT

The NARRATOR leans forward, firelight on his face.

NARRATOR
And so Buck passed into the hands of Perrault and Francois, French-Canadian dispatch carriers for the government. On the Narwhal he met Curly, a Newfoundland, and two huskies, Dave and Spitz.

EXT. DYEA BEACH - DAY

Snow falls. The beach is chaos of men, dogs, and sleds. Buck steps onto the white mush and snorts. He licks snow; it bites like fire.

A friendly husky the size of a wolf leaps at Curly without warning. Teeth clip. Thirty huskies circle silently, licking chops. Curly is tumbled, buried under a mass of bodies, torn to pieces.

Spitz runs out his scarlet tongue, laughing. Francois swings an axe, scatters them too late.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
So that was the way. No fair play. Once down, that was the end of you. From that moment Buck hated Spitz with a bitter and deathless hatred.

EXT. YUKON TRAIL - DAY

Buck is harnessed. The sled moves. Dave nips his hindquarters when he errs. Spitz growls reproof from the lead. Francois's whip sings.

They add Billee, the good-natured, and Joe, the sour one. Then Sol-leks, the one-eyed angry one.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Buck learned easily. He learned to stop at "ho," go at "mush," swing wide on bends. T'ree vair' good dogs, Francois said of them.

Weeks pass. The toil hardens Buck. He steals food to survive. His moral nature decays under the law of club and fang.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The facts of life took on a fiercer aspect. He faced it with all the latent cunning of his nature aroused.

EXT. CAMP ON LAKE LE BARGE - NIGHT

A bleak camp on the ice. Driving snow. Buck's nest is stolen by Spitz. They fight. A horde of starving huskies invades, drawn by food scent. They devour the grub. The team dogs fight for their lives. Curly's fate flashes in Buck's mind. He sees Spitz laughing again.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The dominant primordial beast was strong in Buck. He and Spitz were destined to clash for leadership.

EXT. YUKON TRAIL - DAY

The team pulls as one under Buck's growing defiance. He interferes when Spitz would punish the weak. One night after a snowshoe rabbit chase, Buck and Spitz circle in the moonlight, the pack watching silently.

They clash. Fang against fang. Buck feints, uses imagination, breaks Spitz's forelegs. Spitz goes down. The circle closes.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The time had come. It was to the death. Buck stood, the successful champion, the dominant primordial beast who had made his kill and found it good.

INT. CABIN - NIGHT

The NARRATOR pokes the fire.

NARRATOR
Buck became leader. The team recovered its solidarity. They made record time to Dawson and back. But the mail wore them down. They were sold.

EXT. SKAGUAY STREET - DAY

The sled is overloaded, top-heavy. HAL, young and armed, CHARLES, middle-aged with weak eyes and twisted mustache, and MERCEDES, pretty and soft, argue over the load. Citizens warn them.

The dogs, including a gaunt Buck, strain but cannot move the sled at first. Hal whips them mercilessly. Mercedes interferes with tears.

They lurch forward, spill half the load on the turn. The dogs break into a run, scattering the outfit.

EXT. WHITE RIVER CAMP - DAY

The wretched team staggers into camp. Hal beats Buck, who refuses to rise. JOHN THORNTON watches, then springs forward.

THORNTON
If you strike that dog again, I'll kill you.

Hal draws a knife. Thornton knocks it away, cuts Buck's traces. The sled pulls away. It breaks through the ice. Hal, Charles, Mercedes, and the dogs vanish in the yawning hole.

Thornton kneels by Buck.

THORNTON
You poor devil.

Buck licks his hand.

INT. CABIN - NIGHT

The NARRATOR stares into the flames.

NARRATOR
Love, genuine passionate love, was his for the first time. John Thornton had saved his life and was the ideal master.

EXT. THORNTON'S CAMP - DAY

Buck adores Thornton, follows him everywhere. Thornton roughs his head, calls him ill names that are love names. Buck seizes the hand fiercely in play.

Thornton bets a thousand dollars. A sled loaded with a thousand pounds of flour stands frozen. The crowd gathers.

THORNTON
(whispering in Buck's ear)
As you love me, Buck. As you love me.

Buck tightens the traces, then lunges. The sled breaks out, moves steadily the hundred yards. The crowd roars. Thornton falls to his knees, head against Buck's, tears streaming, cursing him lovingly.

EXT. EASTERN WILDERNESS - DAY

Months later. Thornton, Hans, and Pete pan gold by a stream. Buck lies by the fire, dreaming of the hairy man, of the call.

The call sounds in the forest. Buck wanders, runs with a timber wolf, but returns to Thornton.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He was a thing of the wild, come in from the wild to sit by John Thornton's fire. But the call was sounding, and he felt compelled to answer.

EXT. YEEHAT CAMP - NIGHT

The YEEHAT INDIANS dance around the wreckage of Thornton's spruce-bough lodge. Thornton lies dead by the pool. Skeet and Nig are slain by arrows.

Buck arrives, sees the bodies. A roar rises from him. He launches like a hurricane, rips the Yeehat chief's throat, tears through them in frenzy. Arrows fly. They shoot each other in panic. The survivors flee, calling him the Evil Spirit.

Buck stands over Thornton's body, then raises his head and howls long and wolflike.

INT. CABIN - NIGHT

The NARRATOR closes the book.

NARRATOR
The years were not many when the Yeehats noted a change in the breed of timber wolves, with splashes of brown and a rift of white. They tell of a Ghost Dog that runs at the head of the pack. He is the dominant primordial beast, answering the call of the wild.

FADE OUT.

THE END.
